WEBVTT

00:00:00.263 --> 00:00:02.201
Last time, we turned the equation around.

00:00:02.745 --> 00:00:05.101
Molar mass equals mass divided by moles.

00:00:05.646 --> 00:00:07.518
The mass is easy, because a balance gives

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:07.861
it to us.

00:00:08.486 --> 00:00:09.982
The hard part is the moles.

00:00:10.508 --> 00:00:12.354
You can't weigh them, and you can't count

00:00:12.375 --> 00:00:12.956
the particles.

00:00:13.582 --> 00:00:14.150
But there's a trick.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.025
If the substance is a gas, or makes

00:00:17.066 --> 00:00:19.532
a gas, its volume can do the counting.

00:00:20.368 --> 00:00:23.217
At the same temperature and pressure, equal volumes

00:00:23.259 --> 00:00:25.405
of any gas hold the same number of

00:00:25.486 --> 00:00:25.907
particles.

00:00:26.651 --> 00:00:29.018
That's because gas particles are so far apart

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:31.185
that the volume depends on how many there

00:00:31.347 --> 00:00:33.051
are, not on how big they are.

00:00:33.734 --> 00:00:35.918
At twenty-five degrees and a hundred kilopascals,

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:39.184
one mole of any gas takes up twenty-four

00:00:39.225 --> 00:00:40.467
point seven nine litres.

00:00:41.091 --> 00:00:42.946
But warm a gas and it expands.

00:00:43.451 --> 00:00:44.758
Squeeze it and it shrinks.

00:00:45.363 --> 00:00:47.374
So you must measure the temperature and pressure,

00:00:47.716 --> 00:00:48.480
and keep them steady.

00:00:49.271 --> 00:00:50.160
First, an element.

00:00:50.788 --> 00:00:53.466
Magnesium isn't a gas, but it makes one.

00:00:54.111 --> 00:00:57.504
Magnesium reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid to make

00:00:57.625 --> 00:00:58.448
hydrogen gas.

00:00:59.051 --> 00:01:02.044
The equation says one magnesium atom gives one

00:01:02.065 --> 00:01:03.229
hydrogen molecule.

00:01:03.893 --> 00:01:06.323
So the moles of hydrogen collected equal the

00:01:06.364 --> 00:01:08.071
moles of magnesium that reacted.

00:01:08.775 --> 00:01:10.920
The ribbon is rubbed with emery paper to

00:01:10.981 --> 00:01:13.528
remove its oxide coating, then weighed on a

00:01:13.589 --> 00:01:15.694
balance that reads to a thousandth of a

00:01:15.735 --> 00:01:16.015
gram.

00:01:16.541 --> 00:01:18.005
The ribbon sits at the mouth of a

00:01:18.045 --> 00:01:20.453
burette, turned upside down and full of liquid.

00:01:21.095 --> 00:01:23.446
As the acid reaches it, hydrogen bubbles up

00:01:23.507 --> 00:01:25.415
and collects at the top, pushing the liquid

00:01:25.436 --> 00:01:25.636
down.

00:01:26.439 --> 00:01:28.284
When the ribbon has gone, the gas takes

00:01:28.344 --> 00:01:30.590
up forty-two point two millilitres.

00:01:31.151 --> 00:01:33.204
The room is at twenty-five degrees and a

00:01:33.245 --> 00:01:34.230
hundred kilopascals.

00:01:35.173 --> 00:01:36.003
Now the calculation.

00:01:36.649 --> 00:01:40.055
Change forty-two point two millilitres to litres, then

00:01:40.096 --> 00:01:42.199
divide by twenty-four point seven nine.

00:01:42.702 --> 00:01:45.567
That's one point seven zero times ten to

00:01:45.608 --> 00:01:48.092
the minus three moles of hydrogen, so the

00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:49.274
same amount of magnesium.

00:01:50.139 --> 00:01:52.794
Molar mass equals mass divided by moles.

00:01:53.358 --> 00:01:55.982
Zero point zero four two grams, divided by

00:01:56.123 --> 00:01:57.985
one point seven zero times ten to the

00:01:58.026 --> 00:02:01.171
minus three, is twenty-four point seven grams per

00:02:01.192 --> 00:02:01.411
mole.

00:02:02.055 --> 00:02:04.913
The accepted value is twenty-four point three one.

00:02:05.417 --> 00:02:06.883
That's close, but a little high.

00:02:07.878 --> 00:02:08.461
Quick prediction.

00:02:09.026 --> 00:02:12.104
Suppose some hydrogen escaped before it was collected.

00:02:12.668 --> 00:02:14.294
Would the molar mass come out too high,

00:02:14.697 --> 00:02:15.299
or too low?

00:02:18.797 --> 00:02:19.527
Too high.

00:02:20.336 --> 00:02:22.987
Less gas means a smaller volume, so fewer

00:02:23.069 --> 00:02:23.911
moles are counted.

00:02:24.636 --> 00:02:27.328
Dividing the same mass by fewer moles gives

00:02:27.369 --> 00:02:28.393
a bigger molar mass.

00:02:29.258 --> 00:02:30.964
An oxide coating does the same thing.

00:02:31.467 --> 00:02:33.012
It adds to the mass on the balance,

00:02:33.314 --> 00:02:35.500
but it makes no hydrogen, so the molar

00:02:35.521 --> 00:02:36.925
mass comes out high again.

00:02:37.709 --> 00:02:39.532
That's why the ribbon is cleaned, and why

00:02:39.553 --> 00:02:41.216
the gas must be caught from the very

00:02:41.317 --> 00:02:41.917
first bubble.

00:02:42.736 --> 00:02:44.462
There's another limit, and it's the mass.

00:02:45.286 --> 00:02:46.867
On a balance that reads to a hundredth

00:02:46.888 --> 00:02:49.569
of a gram, our ribbon just shows zero

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:50.690
point zero four.

00:02:51.512 --> 00:02:53.493
The real mass could be anywhere from about

00:02:53.554 --> 00:02:56.235
zero point zero three five to zero point

00:02:56.256 --> 00:02:57.236
zero four five,

00:02:57.918 --> 00:02:59.618
so the molar mass could be anywhere from

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:01.179
about twenty-one to twenty-six.

00:03:02.161 --> 00:03:03.827
A balance that reads to a thousandth of

00:03:03.868 --> 00:03:05.393
a gram narrows that down.

00:03:06.218 --> 00:03:08.574
The smallest reading is usually the weak link.

00:03:09.381 --> 00:03:12.429
Now a compound, an unknown liquid that evaporates

00:03:12.609 --> 00:03:12.910
easily.

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:14.919
Chemists call that volatile.

00:03:15.844 --> 00:03:17.976
Weigh a dry flask with a foil cap

00:03:18.117 --> 00:03:19.183
pierced by a pinhole.

00:03:19.965 --> 00:03:21.687
Add a few millilitres of the liquid, and

00:03:21.747 --> 00:03:23.790
stand the flask in a boiling water bath

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:26.274
on a hotplate, because the vapour is flammable

00:03:26.354 --> 00:03:28.016
and must stay away from flames.

00:03:28.998 --> 00:03:31.641
The liquid boils away, and its vapour pushes

00:03:31.682 --> 00:03:33.804
the air out through the pinhole, until the

00:03:33.845 --> 00:03:35.266
flask is full of vapour,

00:03:36.048 --> 00:03:38.050
at the bath's temperature and the room's pressure.

00:03:39.302 --> 00:03:41.128
Then take the flask out and let it

00:03:41.189 --> 00:03:41.409
cool.

00:03:41.991 --> 00:03:44.899
The vapour inside condenses back to liquid, so

00:03:44.979 --> 00:03:47.245
reweighing the dried flask gives the mass of

00:03:47.325 --> 00:03:48.287
vapour that filled it,

00:03:48.809 --> 00:03:50.754
zero point two seven two of a gram.

00:03:51.775 --> 00:03:54.239
Finally, fill the flask with water and measure

00:03:54.259 --> 00:03:56.222
that water, to find the volume the vapour

00:03:56.262 --> 00:03:56.602
took up,

00:03:57.163 --> 00:03:58.425
one hundred and forty millilitres.

00:03:59.636 --> 00:04:01.940
At the bath's temperature, gases spread out more,

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:04.487
so a mole takes up more room, about

00:04:04.588 --> 00:04:07.094
thirty point six litres, using the gas laws

00:04:07.115 --> 00:04:08.277
you'll meet later in this module.

00:04:09.139 --> 00:04:11.180
So the vapour is zero point one four

00:04:11.221 --> 00:04:14.142
zero divided by thirty point six, about four

00:04:14.183 --> 00:04:16.323
point five eight times ten to the minus

00:04:16.344 --> 00:04:16.904
three moles.

00:04:17.866 --> 00:04:19.909
The molar mass is zero point two seven

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:21.392
two divided by that,

00:04:21.994 --> 00:04:24.037
fifty-nine point five grams per mole.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:27.786
Of the likely liquids, propanone is closest, at

00:04:27.828 --> 00:04:28.950
fifty-eight point zero eight.

00:04:30.065 --> 00:04:31.760
This result is a little high too.

00:04:32.384 --> 00:04:33.667
If the outside of the flask is still

00:04:33.727 --> 00:04:35.993
wet from the bath, the water adds mass,

00:04:36.334 --> 00:04:37.798
so the molar mass comes out high.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:41.248
And if some liquid hadn't evaporated, the flask

00:04:41.348 --> 00:04:43.735
holds extra mass, which also pushes it up.

00:04:44.596 --> 00:04:46.564
So both methods use one idea.

00:04:47.127 --> 00:04:47.928
Weigh the substance,

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:50.594
use a gas volume at known temperature and

00:04:50.635 --> 00:04:52.920
pressure to count the moles, then divide.

00:04:53.583 --> 00:04:55.551
The difference is where the gas comes from.

00:04:56.275 --> 00:04:59.450
Magnesium makes hydrogen, so the equation links the

00:04:59.491 --> 00:04:59.992
two amounts.

00:05:00.656 --> 00:05:02.420
The liquid becomes the gas itself,

00:05:02.943 --> 00:05:04.927
so its moles are the vapour's moles.

00:05:05.631 --> 00:05:06.519
Here's a line to remember.

00:05:07.123 --> 00:05:09.330
Weigh it, count it with a gas, then

00:05:09.371 --> 00:05:09.752
divide.

00:05:12.503 --> 00:05:12.947
Your turn.

00:05:14.132 --> 00:05:16.874
Zero point one five zero grams of zinc

00:05:17.275 --> 00:05:21.459
reacts completely with dilute hydrochloric acid, making fifty-six

00:05:21.519 --> 00:05:25.043
point nine millilitres of hydrogen at twenty-five degrees

00:05:25.203 --> 00:05:26.444
and a hundred kilopascals.

00:05:27.226 --> 00:05:28.916
Calculate the molar mass of zinc.

00:05:29.543 --> 00:05:30.474
Pause here to answer.

00:05:41.241 --> 00:05:44.374
Fifty-six point nine millilitres is zero point zero

00:05:44.535 --> 00:05:46.162
five six nine litres.

00:05:46.764 --> 00:05:49.686
Divided by twenty-four point seven nine, that's two

00:05:49.766 --> 00:05:51.848
point two nine five times ten to the

00:05:51.988 --> 00:05:54.669
minus three moles of hydrogen, and of zinc,

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:56.511
because the equation is one to one.

00:05:57.111 --> 00:05:59.253
So the molar mass is zero point one

00:05:59.313 --> 00:06:02.415
five zero divided by that, sixty-five point four

00:06:02.455 --> 00:06:03.176
grams per mole.

00:06:03.858 --> 00:06:06.104
Leaving the volume in millilitres gives a tiny,

00:06:06.185 --> 00:06:07.027
impossible answer.

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:08.617
That's the slip to avoid.

00:06:10.390 --> 00:06:10.874
Last one.

00:06:11.999 --> 00:06:15.003
In the volatile liquid method, a student reweighs

00:06:15.024 --> 00:06:17.567
the cooled flask without drying its outside.

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:20.732
Explain the effect on the calculated molar mass.

00:06:21.475 --> 00:06:22.362
Pause here to answer.

00:06:33.081 --> 00:06:35.410
The molar mass comes out too high.

00:06:36.132 --> 00:06:38.218
The water on the outside is weighed as

00:06:38.278 --> 00:06:40.102
if it were vapour, so the mass of

00:06:40.163 --> 00:06:41.486
vapour is too big.

00:06:42.249 --> 00:06:44.847
The volume, and so the moles, don't change.

00:06:45.491 --> 00:06:47.562
A bigger mass divided by the same moles

00:06:47.743 --> 00:06:49.331
gives a bigger molar mass.

00:06:50.235 --> 00:06:52.637
Saying it's too low, because water dilutes the

00:06:52.678 --> 00:06:54.559
liquid, is the classic slip.

00:06:56.569 --> 00:06:58.116
One run gives one number.

00:06:58.619 --> 00:07:00.805
So a class did the magnesium method five

00:07:00.866 --> 00:07:03.152
times, with five different masses of ribbon.

00:07:03.876 --> 00:07:04.748
Here's what they wrote down.

00:07:05.476 --> 00:07:08.438
Mixed units, missing labels, and a note about

00:07:08.519 --> 00:07:09.359
bubbles getting away.

00:07:10.321 --> 00:07:12.623
Next time, we'll turn raw readings like these

00:07:12.784 --> 00:07:14.526
into a table and a graph we can

00:07:14.687 --> 00:07:15.267
actually use.

00:07:18.061 --> 00:07:18.884
Here are the key notes.

00:07:19.407 --> 00:07:20.955
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:20.995 --> 00:07:21.417
to copy them.
